<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  "طلباتي" — the order history. Figma 562:17527.

  Core's block, core's pager, core's collection. What changes is the template
  and the view model handed to it.

  The pager stays. Figma draws a single card because the mock has one order;
  a shopper with forty needs the pagination core already provides, and the
  alternative — rendering every order a customer has ever placed, each with a
  product-image lookup — is the kind of thing that works in a demo and times
  out in year three.

  ===========================================================================
  WHY setTemplate IS AN <action> AND NOT THE `template` ATTRIBUTE (2026-09-03)
  ===========================================================================
  Because Magento_OrderCancellationUi wins otherwise, and did — the deployed
  store rendered core's `<table class="data table">` from
  `Magento_OrderCancellationUi::order/history.phtml` no matter where this
  handle lived.

  That module ships its own view/frontend/layout/sales_order_history.xml
  (Magento 2.4.7+) which re-templates the SAME block:

      <referenceBlock name="sales.order.history">
          <action method="setTemplate">
              <argument name="template" xsi:type="string">
                  Magento_OrderCancellationUi::order/history.phtml
              </argument>
          </action>
      </referenceBlock>

  An `<action>` is not a merge-time attribute — it is a deferred method call.
  Magento\Framework\View\Layout\Generator\Block::process() generates every
  block first (applying each one's `template` ATTRIBUTE at line ~233) and only
  then, under the comment "Run all actions after layout initialization", walks
  the collected actions. So a `setTemplate` action ALWAYS overwrites a
  `template` attribute, whichever file declared it, whatever the module
  sequence, and whether it came from a module or a theme.

  This is also the correction to a long note that used to sit here. It
  concluded that the theme's Magento_Sales/ directory "is not visible to that
  install, for a reason not present in the repository", and moved the
  presentation into this module on that basis. The directory was fine; the
  attribute was simply losing a race it could never win, and it would have
  lost the same race from the theme. Nothing about that diagnosis was
  reproducible, which is the tell — this one is: it is in the framework's own
  generator, in order, on lines you can read.

  TWO THINGS MAKE THIS DETERMINISTIC:

    1. An `<action>` here, so both modules are playing the same game.
    2. `Magento_OrderCancellationUi` in this module's <sequence> (etc/module.xml),
       so this file merges AFTER theirs and its action therefore runs last.
       Actions execute in merged-document order, and module layout files are
       collected in dependency-sorted module order — without the sequence the
       winner would depend on alphabetical resolution, i.e. on luck.

  WHAT IS GIVEN UP: the cancel-order link core's template renders inside the
  history table. Figma's order card (562:17887) has two actions, تتبع الطلب
  and عرض المنتج, and no cancel — and the capability is not lost, because
  Magento_OrderCancellationUi also puts its button on the ORDER page via
  sales_order_view.xml, which is where a shopper cancelling one order is
  already standing. If the design later wants it on the card, it needs a Figma
  frame and then a `<referenceBlock>`-supplied view model here, not the whole
  core table back.
-->
<page xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:View/Layout/etc/page_configuration.xsd">
    <body>
        <referenceBlock name="sales.order.history">
            <action method="setTemplate">
                <argument name="template" xsi:type="string">Spartrak_CustomerAccount::order/history.phtml</argument>
            </action>
            <arguments>
                <argument name="order_view" xsi:type="object">Spartrak\CustomerAccount\ViewModel\OrderView</argument>
            </arguments>
        </referenceBlock>
    </body>
</page>
